{'type': 'text', 'text': '{
  "stdout": "\nThis work highlights the importance of automated security testing in the ML lifecycle and provides tools for researchers and practitioners to identify and mitigate safety issues in deployed systems.\n\n### 2.4 Synthesis\n\nThese three works represent complementary approaches to building trustworthy ML systems. The residual learning architecture provides the foundation for powerful models that must be made trustworthy; comprehensive assessment frameworks like DecodingTrust provide methodologies for evaluating trustworthiness; and automated red-teaming tools like RedCodeAgent provide means for proactively identifying vulnerabilities. Together, these contributions illustrate the multifaceted nature of trustworthy machine learning research.\n\n## 3. Conclusion\n\nTrustworthy machine learning has become an essential research area as AI systems grow more powerful and more widely deployed. This paper has surveyed key advances across multiple dimensions of trustworthiness, highlighting foundational contributions in deep learning architecture, comprehensive evaluation frameworks, and automated safety testing.\n\nKey takeaways from our survey include:\n\n1. **Architecture matters**: Foundational advances in deep learning architecture, such as residual learning (arXiv:1512.03385), enable the construction of powerful models that must be made trustworthy.\n\n2. **Comprehensive evaluation is essential**: Single-metric evaluations are insufficient for assessing trustworthiness. Frameworks like DecodingTrust (arXiv:2306.11698) demonstrate the importance of multi-dimensional assessment across truthfulness, fairness, robustness, and other key properties.\n\n3. **Proactive testing is critical**: Automated red-teaming methodologies, as demonstrated in RedCodeAgent (arXiv:2510.02609), provide essential tools for identifying vulnerabilities before deployment in real-world systems.\n\n4. **Interdisciplinary collaboration is necessary**: Trustworthy ML requires input from computer science, ethics, law, and domain expertise to address the full spectrum of trustworthiness concerns.\n\nDespite significant progress, many challenges remain. These include developing efficient methods for continuous monitoring of deployed models, addressing emergent safety issues in increasingly complex systems, and establishing regulatory frameworks that protect public welfare without stifling innovation. We believe that continued progress in trustworthy machine learning will require sustained effort from researchers across diverse disciplines, working together to ensure that AI systems are not only powerful but also safe, fair, and aligned with human values.\n\n## References\n\n[1] Kaiming He, Xiangyu Zhang, Shaoqing Ren, and Jian Sun. \"Deep Residual Learning for Image Recognition.\" *arXiv preprint arXiv:1512.03385*, 2015.\n\n[2] Boxin Wang, Weixin Chen, Hengzhi Pei, et al. \"DecodingTrust: A Comprehensive Assessment of Trustworthiness in GPT Models.\" *arXiv preprint arXiv:2306.11698*, 2023.\n\n[3] Chengquan Guo, Chulin Xie, Yu Yang, et al. \"RedCodeAgent: Automatic Red-teaming Agent against Diverse Code Agents.\" *arXiv preprint arXiv:2510.02609*, 2025.\n",
